<p><strong>The J.H. Newton Home</strong></p>
<p><strong>211 6th Ave NW</strong></p>
<p>Mr. and Mrs. {% person "john-henry-newton" %}John Henry Newton{% endperson %} built this home in 1908. They made Mandan their home for over 40 years, residing there until John’s death, at which time {% person "louise-cornish-newton" %}Mrs. Newton{% endperson %} made her home in Bismarck.</p>
<p>At the age of 13, John arrived in Bismarck from Vermont with his parents. He served as a telegraph operator at {% place "camp-hancock" %}Fort Hancock{% endplace %} in Bismarck and then with {% place "western-union" %}Western Union{% endplace %}, where he worked with Bismarck’s first telegraph operator, {% person "j-m-carnahan" %}J. M. Carnahan{% endperson %}, who sent the account of the Custer Massacre over the wires to the {% place "new-york-herald" %}New York Herald{% endplace %}.</p>
<p>John’s job with Western Union also took him to Fargo and Missoula. When he returned to Bismarck, he became affiliated with the {% place "north-dakota-milling-association" %}North Dakota Milling Association{% endplace %} and the {% place "missouri-valley-milling-company" %}Missouri Valley Milling Company{% endplace %}, which had mills in Mandan and Bismarck. John later organized the {% place "missouri-valley-grocery-company" %}Missouri Valley Grocery Company{% endplace %} until the firm was sold to {% place "stone-ordean-wells" %}Stone-Ordean Wells{% endplace %} in 1929. John then took over management of the {% place "riverside-gravel-company" %}Riverside Gravel Company{% endplace %} until his retirement in 1950.</p>
<p>John was active in numerous affairs. He served as president of the {% place "mandan-chamber-of-commerce" %}Chamber of Commerce{% endplace %} for twelve years and was largely responsible for the {% place "united-states-dairy-station" %}United States Dairy Station{% endplace %}, which was later discontinued, locating here in 1928.</p>
<p>The current owners, Mr. and Mrs. {% person "ronald-crouse" %}Ronald Crouse{% endperson %}, purchased the home in 1998. They have done some updating and remodeling, however, the home remains as close to original as it did when it was built by John Newton.</p>
